#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d3c8f6-6594-5c76-9c37-6f575efe47ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242606} {:answer/id #uuid "fdba1a33-43da-5263-a38b-1296aed8f51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242607} {:answer/id #uuid "d69ca0f8-6b86-55d2-851e-7767422a5b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242608} {:answer/id #uuid "b314168c-9cf2-5db6-8dda-e5d2c031a75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242609} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffceba5-fa38-52d8-b800-ffb8b2393ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-6,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdba1a33-43da-5263-a38b-1296aed8f51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242607} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf96151-7725-517b-a7f5-3eb1feb4145f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c78b7fa0-e6a0-5d03-8538-f39afab9a911" :question/math-academy-id "q-112815" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $7{x}^{3}$ and $42{x}^{2}$ is $7{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}(x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+6)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-6$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71d201ed-99a4-582c-b72b-bc2f306cac9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242613} {:answer/id #uuid "72b7281f-317a-5e2a-800b-1d1b5acf8d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242614} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9feeb3-0b9e-5722-95c4-f9d5518c061d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242615} {:answer/id #uuid "cc084344-7f6b-5786-b745-8c5b3fece7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242616} {:answer/id #uuid "58f3a0c4-8aea-50ff-93e6-762f0b18d924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f3a0c4-8aea-50ff-93e6-762f0b18d924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242617} :answer-field/id #uuid "69071cb6-d001-5782-aebf-769c85c51a56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd5a6c88-e76c-5708-b3a1-9be6807bc96b" :question/math-academy-id "q-178254" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=27{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-3x-1$ have multiplicity $1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =27{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-3x-1 \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(3x+1)-(3x+1) \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-1)(3x+1) \\\\  & =(3x-1)(3x+1)(3x+1) \\\\  & =(3x-1)(3x+1{)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(3x+1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(3x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de338209-971a-5f0e-a02e-cf383c059be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242620} {:answer/id #uuid "95af5eee-3d4f-5791-add4-f64c3fe5bc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242621} {:answer/id #uuid "d448fb14-b34e-537c-968c-98fd0362973c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242622} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d9c0bb-c2e0-5614-94af-24e5b9f9aea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242623} {:answer/id #uuid "da5421f4-49bc-5fb2-8ee0-8835ea5e40c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95af5eee-3d4f-5791-add4-f64c3fe5bc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242621} :answer-field/id #uuid "8371780f-8517-53a5-9208-bef904697560" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d601c130-3a93-5630-8b35-93cbbfae4d1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-178256" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-5)-25(x-5) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x-5{)}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aec5e48-3253-5141-8e1e-e752fac1a30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aec5e48-3253-5141-8e1e-e752fac1a30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242627} :answer-field/id #uuid "40182997-b4ea-5779-9cc1-9466d7678264" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbb9d4f7-acd8-5a70-85c8-739913bcdc10" :question/math-academy-id "q-271146" :question/problem "The multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$4$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$14$\n$-8$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$4$\n$-12$\n$8$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-3$\n$2$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c07e73a-a1ad-54ab-a548-ab79d134f41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242630} {:answer/id #uuid "140fafc8-f157-5f06-bce3-607c14b160f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242631} {:answer/id #uuid "e117a576-59f6-5ba3-ad77-6152497b48a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242632} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad31517-c336-52ea-8eee-6504780eafa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242633} {:answer/id #uuid "f39195ad-aa7b-5db4-be34-bc6627b9385c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "140fafc8-f157-5f06-bce3-607c14b160f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242631} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffacf150-bd78-5b93-aa64-58b1f56fdc9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9e57a4e-6690-5ba4-a748-36d2c1e1c0d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74233" :question/problem "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$1$\n$1$\n$1$\n$-5$\n$3$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x-1{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$"}]}
